  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nHigh pressure ridging at the sfc and upper level ridging is moving \nover Upper MI this morning, continuing to taper off winds and clear \nlingering lake effect clouds out. Temps have fallen into the upper \n20s to low 30s where the low cigs cleared out quicker. Onshore flow \nand insulating clouds in the east and near Lake Superior has kept \ntemps warmer in the mid 30s to mid 40s. This sfc ridge departs to \nthe southeast today, increasing southwest flow and warming temps \ninto the upper 40s to mid 50s. Mostly dry weather is expected today, \nbut there is a quick hitting slight chance of showers (more likely \nsprinkles) grazing the Keweenaw and northeastern tier of the UP \ncourtesy of a ridge riding shortwave and warm front. Model soundings \nindicate low level dry air will be a limiting factor, thus the \nforecast has no QPF and PoPs are capped at 24%. CAMs only had a few \nhundreths of QPF, mainly over Lake Superior. A cold front presses \nthrough this evening as a clipper low travels just north of Lake \nSuperior, but dry weather persists through Tuesday. Temps bottom out \ntonight in the mid 30s to low 40s, warming up once again between 5 \nto 15 F above normal on Tuesday in the mid 40s to mid 50s.\n\nA longer period of PoPs starts Wednesday as strong upper ridging \nsets up over the western CONUS with troughing over eastern Canada and \nCONUS. A shortwave initially sends a low pressure to the south with \nmost associated precip outside f-gen forced rain showers grazing the \nWI/MI state line Wednesday morning. Then a parade of sharpness drop \nsouth over the UP through Saturday accompanied by cold 850 mb temps \nof -8 to -10 C. This supports lake effect showers, over the north to \nnorth-northeast wind precip belts. Differences remain on the \nthermodynamic profile expected with varying inversion heights \nbetween 5-10kft and amounts of moisture reaching into the DGZ. \nRegardless, some light snow accumulation is anticipated by the end \nof the week away from the lakeshores. Probabilities for 1 inch of \nsnow by 12Z Saturday have increased to 50-90% over the north to \nnorth-northeast wind snowbelts. Increase the total to 4 inches and \nit drops to 25-50% over the higher elevations of the north-central \nUP as well as Gogebic Range and Porcupine mountains. Otherwise temps \nreturn near normal with highs in the 30s to low 40s and lows in the \n20s to low 30s.",
